1. Distributed web infrastructure - www.foobar.com

Flow: DNS resolves www.foobar.com to the load balancer's IP. HAProxy receives every
request and forwards it to one of the two servers. Each server runs Nginx, an
application server and the code base. Writes go to the MySQL Primary on server 1;
the Replica on server 2 receives a copy of every change.

Why each additional element:
- Load balancer (HAProxy): spreads traffic across servers, so no single server is
  overloaded, and stops sending traffic to a server that is down.
- Second server: adds capacity and redundancy; if one web/app server fails, the
  other keeps serving.
- Database Primary-Replica: keeps a copy of the data on a second machine and lets
  reads be spread across both.

Distribution algorithm: Round Robin. HAProxy sends each new request to the next
server in the list in turn (server 1, server 2, server 1, ...), so load is shared
evenly when servers have the same capacity.

Active-Active vs Active-Passive: this setup is Active-Active, because both servers
receive traffic at the same time. In Active-Passive, only one node serves traffic
and the other stays on standby, taking over only if the active one fails.

How a Primary-Replica (Master-Slave) cluster works: the Primary accepts all writes
and records them in its binary log. The Replica reads that log and replays the same
changes, so its data stays a copy of the Primary's (asynchronously, so it can lag a
little).

Primary vs Replica for the application: the application must send all writes
(INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) to the Primary. The Replica is read-only, so it can only
serve reads (SELECT).

Issues:
- SPOF: the load balancer (only one) and the Primary database (if it fails, no more
  writes are possible).
- Security: no firewall, so every port is exposed; no HTTPS, so traffic travels
  unencrypted and can be read or modified in transit.
- No monitoring: nobody is alerted when a server fails, gets overloaded or runs out
  of disk.
